critiques.suggested_action:
1. Build a single-statistic knob table for every iteration-3 arm:
- bench forecast: twin, nonce, novelty, rand1-3, band patch and vetoes, per task;
- locus: ADD and all 15 random directions at both alphas, with pass rates and correct compositions;
- Llama: the matched-norm pushes;
- reach: vetoes, lists and long-form cells.
Apply the class rule of Section 3.2.5, and add the alpha-2 random rows to Table 110.
2. Rewrite the verdict per task, for example: 'On kth smallest and two sum, matched-norm random pushes at the task-specification positions reach Hill-1 above 2 at 0 to 4 points. On substring search the novelty instruction reaches 1.99. On find duplicate, Fibonacci, LIS and topological order, nothing tested does. On the path tasks the best rows (semantic seed, decommitment direction, a random direction) sit near 1.9 to 2.0.'
3. Report how many of the 15 random directions qualify on each task, so the result reads as a property of pushes rather than of one lucky seed.
Expected impact: +1. This removes the contradiction that sets soundness to 1.

critiques.description:
Table 120 and fig_forecast_hits compare top-1 hit rates computed under three different rules for undefined forecasts:
- bench contest: undefined forecasts count as misses;
- exposure contest: an undefined forecast gets 1/k credit for its uniform tie;
- Llama contest: undefined forecasts are dropped (subtraction 4 of 12).
The exposure figure of 0.36 for subtraction, presented as the worst family, is almost entirely tie credit. Subtraction was defined for 4 of 33 shown algorithms. Where defined, it named the landing 4 of 4 times, in both the named and the unnamed arms: median of medians to quickselect, brute force to hash map, label propagation to union find, exponential search to binary search. On the modal bench bans it was defined twice and missed both.

artifacts.summary:
Qwen3-8B (pinned revision, FP8 W8A8 on a 16 GB RTX 2000 Ada, thinking off, T = 1). The test asks whether a plan subspace P_t at the task-specification positions explains which random pushes change the algorithm the model writes. P_t is the span of the centroid differences of 30 forced 'Implement X' paraphrases per algorithm, plus a name-masked twin, at blocks 12-35, on 15 coding tasks. All 4,144 prompts were used. Every predictor was sha256-committed before sampling.

Scale: 13,500 CRN-coupled programs, labelled by runtime tracer + AST, with a blind judge only on disagreement; 2.51 GPU-hours; USD 0.20 of OpenRouter. The engine reproduced the iteration-3 screen slot for slot (180/180 identical programs).

The plan subspace is reliable: split-half principal cosine 0.89-0.97 vs 0.02 for random subspaces.

C2a (primary), DISCONFIRMED. The projection share pi(u) does not rank which of 40 fresh random directions move the algorithm: rho < 0.2 on 6/6 movable tasks; pooled within-task rho 0.03 [-0.03, 0.09]. The masked variant and structured controls also fail. Which directions move is itself highly reproducible (template split-half rho 0.76-0.90), so the null is not noise. Exploratory: the signed progress of the realised displacement toward the runner-up centroid correlates up to 0.57 with the effect.

Escape fractions (confirmed at N = 30), at alpha 1 / alpha 2:
- T03: 0.30 / 0.63 (quickselect, size-k heap);
- T10: 0.38 / 0.73 (DFS);
- T2: 0.18 / 0.20 (Bellman-Ford);
- T05: 0 / 0.13 (brute force);
- T1: 0.05 / 0.13 (DFS);
- T06 and nine other tasks, including the held-out R2: 0.
Landings match the iteration-3 screen.

Decomposition (20 confirmed directions): removing the P_t component (u_perp) or clamping the realised P_t displacement keeps the effect (pooled median R = 1.00 for both), so P_t is not needed. The amplified P_t component alone also escapes for 9 of 20 directions: generic sensitivity.

C2c, DISCONFIRMED by its rule (5/6 tasks indistinguishable from a matched random push), with T10 as a clean exception. At half the distance to the runner-up centroid (0.15 ban units), T10 gives 48% correct DFS vs 0% for the matched random push (24/0 CRN flips, McNemar p = 1.2e-7). The empirical crossing is s = 0.59 vs the predicted 0.5; T03 crosses at 0.85.

C2b fails: pooled rho 0.27. C3b (descriptive): push landings forecast ban landings best (log score -0.76 vs ASSOCIATE -3.46). Movability tracks ban avoidance across 14 tasks (rho 0.86).

Novelty audit: 0 novel algorithms among 10,651 correct push programs.

Files provided:
- method_out.json (exp_gen_sol_out), with datasets c2a_population, escape_9task, decomposition, targeted and c3b_events;
- results/*.json for every analysis;
- figures fig1-fig9;
- predictions/c2_commit.json and the commitments.log hash chain;
- data/samples_raw.jsonl and data/labels.jsonl;
- two independent audits with placebos (0 mismatches);
- reproducibility.md.

critiques.description:
The site conclusions (4.10 item 3; closing paragraphs 1 and 3) say more than the site experiment [ARTIFACT:art_Cx9JOh8soBw7] supports.
- 'At any dose'. The record says the read position and the condition sentence 'changed no algorithm at any dose (0 of 526 and 0 of 527 ...)'. Those counts cover only the per-position doses alpha 1 to 4. The energy-matched read-position arm S1E, reported in Section 4.4, moved two tasks:
  - find duplicate: Floyd cycle detection 6 of 26 correct;
  - connected components: 5 of 28 correct programs off the default.
- 'Located'. The closing says 'Where the choice is made inside the model is located ... The task-specification states carry it'. Noise in generated tokens 1 to 32 (S3) is not inert:
  - it moves four tasks;
  - it has the best 5-point Hill-1 on two sum and find duplicate (Table 143);
  - its price of 1.5 on two sum is 0.12 against 0.53 (Table 144);
  - it flips find duplicate 12:0 over the plan site at alpha 2 (exact McNemar p 0.0005; Table 146).
- The record disagrees with itself. The frozen rule returned PARTIAL, and Section 4.10 lists the site claim under 'Inconclusive' while item 3 states 'The site is the task-specification states' under 'Established'.
What the experiment establishes is narrower:
- among prefill positions, the task-specification states (blocks 0 to 23; blocks 0 to 11 vs 12 to 23 not separated, -0.31 [-0.75, 0.13] in results/transmission.json) are the effective site per unit downstream displacement;
- the choice is still open during the first 32 generated tokens.

The rebuilt knob table counts exposure arms as knobs. An exposure prompt supplies a correct program of the alternative, which is the reason the A* mention is a control (Section 3.2.5). Table 123's verdicts rest on exposure arms on several tasks:
- connected components (unnamed exposure ban of BFS, 1.85);
- longest increasing subsequence (named exposure ban of the Fenwick tree, 1.78);
- first and last position (exposure vetoes, 1.78 to 1.84);
- nonoverlapping intervals (1.26).
Each of these is priced against its own copy twin, in which the shown algorithm is the default.

Iteration 4's plan-noise effect does not replicate in bf16 at near-matched dose. The record leaves the cause open although its own files narrow it.
All of iteration 4's plan-noise positives ran on an FP8 W8A8 engine (per-token activation scales; exp19 README: 'It does not measure an FP8-vs-bf16 gap'): the price claim, the site claim, the same-seed contrast (17.25 of 20), the subspace escape fractions and the frozen screen. In iteration 5's bf16 knob experiment [ARTIFACT:art_jR6Pyy3W9xum], per-sample plan noise gave these off-default shares (cells.json against exp19 alpha_sweep.json):
- connected components: 1/50 at both alphas, against 6/50 and 30/50 in FP8 (Fisher p 4e-11 at alpha 2);
- shortest path: 0/50 and 2/50, against 13/50 and 27/50 (p 1e-8);
- grid: 0.02 against 0.24 at alpha 2;
- kth smallest: 0.08 at a bf16 dose of 1.39 ban units, against 0.28 at 1.00 ban unit in FP8.
The experiment's own results/unit_ratio.json measured the bf16 dose per position at 0.99 (connected components), 0.89 (shortest path), 0.99 (two sum) and 1.39 (kth smallest) ban units per alpha. Even the record's Table 174 ratios (0.75 to 0.97) allow at most a 25% dose shortfall, against a 10- to 30-fold drop in effect. At larger norm the contrast holds as well. The transplant experiment's matched random displacement at lambda 1, mostly above 4 units, moved connected components 0.10 at pass 1.00. FP8 plan noise at alpha 4 moved it 0.64 at pass 0.42.
The engine checks used two directions pre-selected as strong FP8 escapers (9002, 9004), so they cannot speak for the direction population. The record still says movability 'depends on the engine and the unit' and that the cause is 'not established' (5.10, 5.12, closing). It does not report unit_ratio.json, and it leaves iteration-4 items 2 to 4 under 'Established'.
Two iteration-5 comparisons ran in a regime where plan noise was nearly inactive on 4 of 5 decisive tasks: the plan sampler DISCONFIRMED verdict and the movability half. The fig_beta_escape data and 5.10 also contrast FP8 existing-task escape fractions with bf16 new-task ones.
A plausible mechanism, not established: a displacement changes the per-token absmax and hence the FP8 activation scale, adding quantisation error across channels.

The request asks for actual geometry plots in activation space and names geodesic distance and MDS. Iteration 8 produced several geometry figure sets, but only one has a marker: fig_ban_pca_states, the PCA of the 300 forced states. The following have no marker:
- exp44 F3 and F4: the MDS and Isomap plots of that manifold, although 8.8 quotes their stress and neighbour-graph numbers;
- exp44 F5: the per-program states;
- exp39 F8: PCA of the post-fork states (nearest-centroid accuracy 0.69 to 0.975), described in 8.3;
- exp40 F3: MDS, Isomap and PCA of every prompt in the fitted rank-8 handle coordinates (stress-1 0.015 and 0.027), described in 8.4; F3b, the handle axis;
- exp42 F4: block-17 coordinates on the (R1, R2) and (PC1, PC2) planes, which 8.6 never mentions.

dimension_scores.justification:
Three headline conclusions contradict the run's committed outcomes:
- 'the same shape as Gemma's circuit' (8.10, Table 414, closing), against the committed DIFFERENT MOTIF;
- 'a rank-4 handle in both families' (8.4 verdict, closing), against the committed NO HANDLE at Qwen3-8B's fork;
- 'the highest-entropy position ... in every sample' (closing), against 2/50 and 0/37 in Section 8.3.
By the review rule, a conclusion that contradicts the run's own evidence scores 1. The preregistered experiments and their tables are accurate. With the three restatements below, soundness would be 3.

strengths:
Across eight iterations the record covers the whole original request:
- same-seed temperature sweeps with top-p 1 and top-k 10 (question 1a-c);
- the seed strings and instruction wordings;
- the mean-answer hypothesis, tested as base against instruct at the fork;
- BFS/DFS/Dijkstra internals with SAE, NLA, logit and tuned lens, thought anchors, circuits and DAS;
- the top-five prohibition, with survivors placed on a ten-algorithm manifold by geodesic distance, MDS and Isomap.
Every pool was audited for novelty and none produced a novel algorithm (Table 415).

critiques.description:
The user asked that 'the reports need to contain actual geometry plots, actual activation space, not some summary bar charts'. The closing says such plots exist 'on the discipline-by-container frame (iteration 10)'. Section 10.8 has no [FIGURE] marker, although art_ho7tqaOyBjkN rendered:
- the factorial frames (figures/F1_factorial_8b, F2_factorial_32b);
- the Isomap full frame with the placed survivor families (F3_full_frame_8b/32b);
- the container-held-fixed frames as classical MDS of kNN geodesics (F4);
- per-family geodesic violins (F6);
- the family-by-nearest-class placement heatmap (F8).
Section 10.9 places only the dose curve (fig_basew_dose). art_a7CQm-6CXmoJ also rendered the fork states on the readout plane (F5_readout_plane) and a PCA of block-35 outputs at the fork (F6_pca_block35). So the iteration-10 figures the record places are all summary charts, and the closing's statement depends on figures the record never places.
